1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,184
Most people think ear infections only happen to kids.

2
00:00:03,504 --> 00:00:07,069
They don't — and it usually comes down to one tiny tube.

3
00:00:07,389 --> 00:00:11,855
Your Eustachian tube connects your middle ear to your throat and balances pressure.

4
00:00:12,175 --> 00:00:16,373
When it stays blocked, fluid pools behind your eardrum and bacteria move in.

5
00:00:16,693 --> 00:00:20,653
First, riding out a cold or sinus infection instead of treating it.

6
00:00:20,973 --> 00:00:27,441
The longer inflammation lingers, the longer your tube stays shut, and fluid keeps collecting behind your eardrum.

7
00:00:27,761 --> 00:00:31,228
Second, skipping swallowing or yawning during takeoff and landing.

8
00:00:31,548 --> 00:00:37,522
Pressure shifts fast, and without your tube opening to match it, pressure builds and pulls fluid out of the lining.

9
00:00:37,842 --> 00:00:41,857
Finally, regular exposure to cigarette smoke, yours or someone else's.

10
00:00:42,177 --> 00:00:47,983
It damages the tiny cilia that sweep fluid out of your Eustachian tube, so drainage slows down.

11
00:00:48,303 --> 00:00:50,557
Treat colds and sinus infections early.

12
00:00:50,877 --> 00:00:53,822
Chew gum or swallow during takeoff and landing.

13
00:00:54,142 --> 00:00:59,863
Keep your distance from smoke, direct or secondhand — your Eustachian tube handles the rest.
